<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ranganathaswamy Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, on an island in the Kaveri River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, is one of the most important shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and among the largest functioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple complexes in the world. Enlarged over centuries by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Vijayanagara, it is enclosed by seven concentric walls with towering gateways.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam.jpg" alt="Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RUPESH MAURYA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranganathaswamy_temple_tiruchirappalli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
